# What does a monopolist do differently?
voices: {"a":"gemini:Achird | curious, open-minded economist host who loves a puzzle; warm, thinks aloud, brisk lively pace (never slow, never rushed); takes objections seriously, never preachy","b":"gemini:Kore | sharp, friendly co-host: asks what a smart listener would, pushes back with real objections, sometimes explains; dry wit, never exaggerated; brisk lively pace, never slow"}
prompt: "Why does a monopolist sell less at a higher price? What is marginal revenue, and why is it below the price?"
next: {"title":"How much does monopoly cost society?","href":"https://www.drawcast.app/#gh=hmelberg/drawcast-library/econ-from-zero/33-how-much-does-monopoly-cost-society.cast"}

## Seven hundred and fifty dollars
use: bar_chart
with:
    labels:
      - Aug 2015
      - Sept 2015
    values:
      - 13.5
      - 750
    value_labels: true
    bar_colors: same
    icons:
      - pill
      - pill
    ylim:
      - 0
      - 1000
    y_label: Price per tablet ($)
    x_label: Daraprim, USA
    gap: 0.5
    label_size: 24
sources:
    - id: daraprim
      title: Turing raises Daraprim price 5,000 percent
      authors: FDAnews; Healio; BioSpace (news reports)
      year: 2015
      finding: Turing Pharmaceuticals bought US rights to Daraprim (pyrimethamine, FDA-approved 1953, standard first treatment for toxoplasmosis) from Impax in August 2015 and within weeks raised the price from $13.50 to $750 per tablet.
      url: https://fdanews.com/articles/173262-turing-raises-daraprim-price-5000-percent

    text only "Only seller" #b5482e at.data [0,600] font_size 40 hidden true
    text x55 "× 55" #b5482e at.data [0,600] cites ["daraprim"] font_size 60 hidden true
    text why "Why not $75,000?" at.data [0,600] font_size 40 hidden true
    text pq "P or Q — not both" at.data [0,600] font_size 40 hidden true

    card title "Seven hundred and fifty dollars"

What does a firm do differently when nobody can undercut it? Start with a small white pill called Daraprim.
    draw axes

B: It treats toxoplasmosis, a parasite that (@draw bar_1@) can kill people with weak immune systems. Approved in 1953, it cost thirteen dollars f (@point at.ref bar_1 gesture circle@)ifty a tablet in the US in 2015.

That August a company called Turing bought the rights t (@draw only@)o it. Turing was now the only seller.

    erase only

    ask "Drag the second bar to the price Turing set a few weeks later." intro "Your turn." on bar_2 store g default "100" relative true voice b
        right: Well judged: you said {g} dollars, and it was {g.true}.
        wrong: You said {g} dollars; it was {g.true}.

Seven hundred and fifty dollars a tablet. Fifty (@draw x55@)-five times as much, overnight.

The naive story writes itself: a monopolist charges whatever it likes.
    highlight bar_2 effect box

    erase x55

B: Then why stop at seven fifty?  (@draw why@)Why not seventy-five thousand?

Because a monopolist can choose its price, but not its buyers. At a higher price, fewer buy.
    highlight bar_2 effect pulse

    erase why

B: So it picks the price, and the buyers pick  (@draw pq@)how many. It can't pick both.

Somewhere between selling lots cheaply and selling nothing at a fortune lies the price that earns the most. Finding it is the whole puzzle.
    highlight pq effect underline

    pause 0.4

## One more haul-out
domain: {"x":[0,10],"y":[0,1400]}
vars:
    'n':
      value: 4
      min: 1
      max: 10
      step: 1
    P:
      expr: 1300 - 100*n
    R:
      expr: n*(1300 - 100*n)
    MR:
      expr: n*(1300 - 100*n) - (n-1)*(1300 - 100*(n-1))
    MRabs:
      expr: abs(n*(1300 - 100*n) - (n-1)*(1300 - 100*(n-1)))

    axes axes x_label "Haul-outs a week (Q)" y_label "Price ($)" cross corner ticks.x [2,4,6] ticks.y true hidden true
    curve D #b5482e expr "1300 - 100*x" x_from 0 x_to 10 hidden true
    label label_D "D" right #b5482e attach_to D hidden true
    curve ptop #8f887c expr "1300 - 100*n" x_from 0 x_to 4 bind.x_to n dash true hidden true
    curve base #3d3833 expr "0" x_from 0 x_to 10 hidden true
    dot pt #3d3833 at.on D at.x 4 bind {"at.x":"n"} hidden true
    region rev x_from 0 x_to.ref pt between ["ptop","base"] style.fill #f2c14e hidden true
    text readout "Q = {n}  ·  P = ${P:0,}  ·  Revenue = ${R:0,}" at.data [7.2,1250] font_size 34 hidden true
    scale guess x 560 y 470 width 360 label "Revenue added by haul-out 5" unit $ min 0 max 1000 value 400 hidden true
    curve l900 #8f887c expr "900" x_from 0 x_to 4 hidden true
    region gain x_from 4 x_to 5 between ["ptop","base"] style.fill #87a878 hidden true
    text label_gain "+$800" #3d6b3a at.data [4.5,420] font_size 36 hidden true
    region loss x_from 0 x_to 4 between ["l900","ptop"] style.fill #c96567 hidden true
    text label_loss "−$400" #b5482e at.data [2,990] font_size 36 hidden true
    math mr at.data [7.2,1150] tex "\\text{MR} = \\$400 < P = \\$800" size 34 colors {"\\text{MR}":"#2f6b8f"} hidden true
    text mrlive "MR = ${MR:0,}" #2f6b8f at.data [7.2,1130] font_size 34 bind {"style.opacity":"max(0, min(1, MR + 1))"} style.opacity 1 hidden true
    text mrneg "MR = −${MRabs:0,}" #2f6b8f at.data [7.2,1130] font_size 34 bind {"style.opacity":"max(0, min(1, 0 - MR))"} style.opacity 0 hidden true

    card title "One more haul-out"

B: To find that price, take a monopoly small enough to see: Harbourtown's boatyard, the only place in  (@highlight D@)town that can haul a boat out of the water.
    draw axes base D label_D

B: Say, with illustrative numbers, it can sell fo (@draw pt ptop rev readout@)ur haul-outs a week at nine hundred dollars each. Its revenue is this rectangle: three thousand six hundred.

And to sell a fifth?
    point at.data [5,800] gesture circle

    keep rev ptop

B: It has to cut the price to eight hundred. For everyone, not just the new customer.
    animate n 5 duration 2.5

    erase readout

    draw guess

    ask "Click the line at your guess." on guess store g voice b
        intro: Guess: how much does that fifth haul-out add to the yard's revenue?
        right: Well done: you said {g}, and it is {g.true}.
        wrong: You said {g}; it is {g.true}. Most people say eight hundred.

    erase guess

    draw readout

Four hundred doll (@draw gain label_gain@)ars. The new boat pays eight hundred...

B: ...but the four boats it al (@draw l900 loss label_loss@)ready had now pay a hundred less each. That's four hundred gone.

    erase readout

So one more sale adds its price, minus what t (@draw mr@)he price cut costs on all the others. That is marginal revenue, MR.

B: For a firm that must cut its price to sell more, MR is below the price. A small competitive firm sells at the market price, so for it the two are equal.
    highlight mr effect box

    erase mr gain label_gain loss label_loss l900 rev_ghost ptop_ghost

    draw readout mrlive mrneg

Try it: slide the number of haul-outs and watch the rectangle. Past six or seven a week, selling more brings in less.
    explore params ["n"] watch "The haul-outs sweep from ten down to one and back: the rectangle swells, tops out at six or seven, then shrinks as MR turns negative."

Revenue tops out at four thousand two hundred, at six or seven haul-outs. At eight, MR is minus two hundred.
    animate n 8 duration 3

B: So for a monopolist the next sale is always worth less than its price. That one gap is the whole difference.

## Where MR meets MC
use: firm_cost_curves
with:
    mode: monopoly
    show:
      - demand
      - mr
      - mc
    box:
      x: 20
      'y': 160
      w: 600
      h: 500
sources:
    - id: cournot
      title: Recherches sur les principes mathématiques de la théorie des richesses
      authors: Cournot, A. A.
      year: 1838
      finding: 'First mathematical treatment of monopoly: the seller maximises revenue minus cost, setting the derivative of revenue equal to marginal cost.'

    dot cross #3d3833 at.data [42.7,21.7] hidden true
    arrow up cross -> q_star #8f887c hidden true
    dot comp #2f6b8f at.data [54.1,46.7] hidden true
    label label_comp "Competitive" right #2f6b8f attach_to comp hidden true
    text cue "Less, dearer" #b5482e x 810 y 520 font_size 40 hidden true

    card title "Where MR meets MC"

Each extra haul-out earns less than its price. Now set that against what it costs. Here is the yard's demand, smoothed over many boa (@draw mr label_mr@)ts, with MR below it, falling twice as fast.
    draw axes demand label_demand

B: And its marginal  (@draw mc label_mc@)cost, MC: what one more haul-out costs in hours, cradles and diesel.

So where does the yard stop? My first guess would be where de (@point at.data [54.1,46.7] gesture circle@)mand meets MC, like a competitive market.

    quiz "Where does a profit-seeking yard produce?" voice b
        right: Where MR meets MC. Left of it, the next haul-out adds more to revenue than to cost; right of it, less.
        wrong: Weigh what the next haul-out adds to revenue against its cost: MR meets MC.
        * Where demand meets MC
        + Where MR meets MC
        * Where demand is highest

    draw cross

Then the pr (@draw up q_star label_q_star p_star label_p_star@)ice comes straight off the demand curve above that point: P star, w (@highlight p_star label_p_star@)hat buyers will pay for that many.

B: A competitive mark (@draw comp label_comp@)et would go on to where demand meets MC. More haul-outs, at a lower price.

So that's the answer. A monopolist sells less (@draw cue@) at a higher price, not out of spite, but because every extra (@point at.ref comp gesture tap@) sale drags down the price on all the others.

    erase cue

B: Antoine Augustin Cournot worked this out in 1838, in one of the first books to do economics with mathematics.
    portrait cournot_p x 810 y 330 width 190 of "Antoine Augustin Cournot" cameo true

    erase cournot_p

The haul-outs in between are boats worth fixing at what they cost, left unfixed. What that costs a town is a question of its own.
    highlight q_star comp effect circle

## Does anyone compute MR?
use: firm_cost_curves
with:
    mode: monopoly
    show:
      - demand
      - mr
      - mc
    box:
      x: 20
      'y': 160
      w: 600
      h: 500
sources:
    - id: hallhitch
      title: Price Theory and Business Behaviour
      authors: Hall, R. L. & Hitch, C. J.
      year: 1939
      finding: 'Questionnaire interviews with 38 (unrepresentative) British firms: most said they set prices on a full-cost basis, average cost plus a margin for profit, not by equating marginal revenue and marginal cost.'
    - id: machlup
      title: Marginal Analysis and Empirical Research
      authors: Machlup, F.
      year: 1946
      finding: 'Reply to Lester and the full-cost critics: business people need not compute marginal revenue and cost to act as if they did, like a driver deciding to overtake a truck without calculating speeds and distances. American Economic Review 36: 519–554.'
    - id: dleu
      title: The Rise of Market Power and the Macroeconomic Implications
      authors: De Loecker, J., Eeckhout, J. & Unger, G.
      year: 2020
      finding: Sales-weighted average markup of US publicly listed firms rose from 21% above marginal cost in 1980 to 61% in 2016, driven by the upper tail; the median is unchanged.
      doi: 10.1093/qje/qjz041
    - id: traina
      title: Is Aggregate Market Power Increasing? Production Trends Using Financial Statements
      authors: Traina, J.
      year: 2018
      finding: Counting selling, general and administrative costs as variable costs, the measured rise in US markups is much smaller.
takeaways:
    - Each sale cuts its own price
    - It stops where MR meets MC
    - Less sold, at a higher price

    dot cross #3d3833 at.data [42.7,21.7] hidden true
    dot comp #2f6b8f at.data [54.1,46.7] hidden true
    label label_comp "Competitive" right #2f6b8f attach_to comp hidden true
    text full "Cost + margin" #b5482e cites ["hallhitch"] x 810 y 540 font_size 40 hidden true
    text asif "“As if”" #2f6b8f cites ["machlup"] x 810 y 440 font_size 44 hidden true
    text cons "Theory: consensus" #3d6b3a x 810 y 560 font_size 36 hidden true
    text mk "Markups 21% → 61%" cites ["dleu"] x 810 y 460 font_size 34 hidden true
    text cont "Evidence: contested" #b5482e cites ["traina"] x 810 y 380 font_size 36 hidden true

    card title "Does anyone compute MR?"

B: Doesn't all of that assume the yard actually knows its MR curve? Does any real firm compute one?
    draw axes demand label_demand mr label_mr mc label_mc cross q_star label_q_star p_star label_p_star comp label_comp

In 1939 two Oxford economists, Robert Hall and Charles Hitch, a (@draw full@)sked thirty-eight firms how they set prices. Most said: cost, plus a margin.

B: No marginal revenue, no marginal cost. That started a quarrel that ran for a decade.
    fade mr label_mr mc label_mc cross to 0.3

Fritz Machlup's reply, in 1946: a driver overtaking a tru (@draw asif@)ck doesn't compute speeds and distances either. He acts as if he did.

B: And a margin that ignores the buyers gets corrected by trial and error. So cost-plus c (@highlight cross q_star effect circle@)an end up close to where MR meets MC.
    fade mr label_mr mc label_mc cross to 1

    erase full asif

One more assumption: one price for all. A firm that can charge each buyer differently plays another game, and that's a story of its own.
    highlight p_star label_p_star

How sure are we? That a one-price monopolist sells less at a h (@draw cons@)igher price is textbook th (@highlight q_star p_star@)eory: consensus.

B: How much market power real firms have is another matter. One study found markups of US (@draw mk@) listed firms rose from 21 percent above marginal cost in 1980 to 61 percent in 2016.

Others count costs differently and find a much smaller ris (@draw cont@)e. That one is contested.

    erase cons mk cont

    quiz "A town's only theatre sells 100 seats at $30, or 110 if it cuts the price to $28 for everyone. What do the extra 10 seats add to revenue?"
        right: $80. The ten new seats bring $280, but the hundred earlier buyers now pay $2 less each, $200 in all.
        wrong: Ten new seats bring $280, the hundred earlier buyers pay $200 less: $80.
        * $280
        + $80
        * $28

    quiz "Cheaper ingredients cut a patented drug's marginal cost. What does its one-price monopolist do?" voice b
        right: Cuts the price and sells more. MC drops below MR, so more units pay their way, and selling them takes a lower price.
        wrong: MR meets the lower MC further right: it cuts the price and sells more.
        * Keeps the price and pockets the saving
        + Cuts the price and sells more
        * Raises the price

So what does a monopolist do differently? It still faces its buyers. It just knows that each extra sale cuts its own price, so it stops early, and charges more for less.

B: Rule of thumb or calculus, trial and error lands it on the same spot: where the next sale no longer pays for itself.

That's the gap to look for in any sole seller. Fewer sold, at a higher price than competition would set, whether it's a pill or the only boatyard in town.

B: Seven hundred and fifty dollars a tablet wasn't whatever Turing liked. It was a bet on how many would still have to buy.

