    '|a||Fritz Machlup''s reply, in 1946: a driver overtaking a truck doesn''t compute speeds and distances either. He acts as if he did.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 9320
      voice: 'gemini:Achird | curious, open-minded economist host who loves a puzzle; warm, thinks aloud, brisk lively pace (never slow, never rushed); takes objections seriously, never preachy #ctx:djvvzg'
    '|b||And a margin that ignores the buyers gets corrected by trial and error. So cost-plus can end up close to where MR meets MC.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 8160
      voice: 'gemini:Kore | sharp, friendly co-host: asks what a smart listener would, pushes back with real objections, sometimes explains; dry wit, never exaggerated; brisk lively pace, never slow #ctx:1i1i16p'
    '|a||One more assumption: one price for all. A firm that can charge each buyer differently plays another game, and that''s a story of its own.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 8840
      voice: 'gemini:Achird | curious, open-minded economist host who loves a puzzle; warm, thinks aloud, brisk lively pace (never slow, never rushed); takes objections seriously, never preachy #ctx:arlyj0'
    '|a||How sure are we? That a one-price monopolist sells less at a higher price is textbook theory: consensus.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 7240
      voice: 'gemini:Achird | curious, open-minded economist host who loves a puzzle; warm, thinks aloud, brisk lively pace (never slow, never rushed); takes objections seriously, never preachy #ctx:naxffk'
    '|b||How much market power real firms have is another matter. One study found markups of US listed firms rose from 21 percent above marginal cost in 1980 to 61 percent in 2016.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 12960
      voice: 'gemini:Kore | sharp, friendly co-host: asks what a smart listener would, pushes back with real objections, sometimes explains; dry wit, never exaggerated; brisk lively pace, never slow #ctx:19kojx6'
    '|a||Others count costs differently and find a much smaller rise. That one is contested.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 5080
      voice: 'gemini:Achird | curious, open-minded economist host who loves a puzzle; warm, thinks aloud, brisk lively pace (never slow, never rushed); takes objections seriously, never preachy #ctx:zr92co'
    '|a||A town''s only theatre sells 100 seats at $30, or 110 if it cuts the price to $28 for everyone. What do the extra 10 seats add to revenue?':
      mp3: 
      ms: 11800
      voice: 'gemini:Achird | curious, open-minded economist host who loves a puzzle; warm, thinks aloud, brisk lively pace (never slow, never rushed); takes objections seriously, never preachy #ctx:a6lp19'
    '|a||$80. The ten new seats bring $280, but the hundred earlier buyers now pay $2 less each, $200 in all.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 9560
      voice: gemini:Achird | curious, open-minded economist host who loves a puzzle; warm, thinks aloud, brisk lively pace (never slow, never rushed); takes objections seriously, never preachy
    '|b||Cheaper ingredients cut a patented drug''s marginal cost. What does its one-price monopolist do?':
      mp3: 
      ms: 6160
      voice: 'gemini:Kore | sharp, friendly co-host: asks what a smart listener would, pushes back with real objections, sometimes explains; dry wit, never exaggerated; brisk lively pace, never slow'
    '|b||Cuts the price and sells more. MC drops below MR, so more units pay their way, and selling them takes a lower price.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 8320
      voice: 'gemini:Kore | sharp, friendly co-host: asks what a smart listener would, pushes back with real objections, sometimes explains; dry wit, never exaggerated; brisk lively pace, never slow'
    '|a||So what does a monopolist do differently? It still faces its buyers. It just knows that each extra sale cuts its own price, so it stops early, and charges more for less.':
